Aspiration Pneumonia Risk
Respiratory System
Choking after swallowing, Dysphagia history, Dependent lobe consolidation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-007
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of coughing fits, choking during feeding, and subsequent dyspnea requiring urgent respiratory assessment.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of dyspnea and persistent coughing following a meal in a frail patient with known neurological deficits. Urgent clinical assessment was required to evaluate for acute airway obstruction and early signs of chemical pneumonitis before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He started choking right after dinner and now he cannot catch his breath and keeps coughing up foul stuff." (Reported by nursing staff)
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began abruptly 2 hours ago during the evening meal. The patient was observed coughing and choking immediately following swallowing. Staff note a history of severe dysphagia secondary to a prior stroke. Over the last hour, there have been episodes of sudden onset dyspnea and expectoration of foul-smelling purulent sputum. Frequent throat clearing during meals has been noted over the past week, alongside unintended weight loss due to feeding difficulties.
Functional Status: Bedbound; requires full assistance with feeding and activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent aspiration; compromised oral hygiene noted by staff.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 91% on room air | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Frail, visibly distressed, coughing intermittently, tachypneic.
Physical Exam: Oral cavity reveals poor dentition and compromised oral hygiene. Chest auscultation reveals focal coarse crackles and decreased breath sounds localized strictly to the right lower dependent lung zone. Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia. The clinical picture strongly aligns with dependent lobe consolidation secondary to aspiration.
Environmental Observations: Patient found lying flat in bed; head of bed not elevated.
Medication Reconciliation: Thickened fluids and puréed diet orders noted in the chart but reportedly poorly tolerated recently.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Aspiration Pneumonia Risk - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Foreign Body Airway Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient is able to vocalize, cough forcefully, and air entry is present bilaterally (though reduced at right base).

Acute Pulmonary Edema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Crackles are strictly focal/unilateral rather than bilateral and symmetric; no elevated JVP.

Viral Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden onset immediately following a meal, localized right basal signs, and foul-smelling sputum.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic signs of an acute aspiration event, including sudden dyspnea and choking after swallowing, with focal right-sided dependent coarse crackles on exam. The presence of a low-grade fever, tachycardia, and hypoxemia strongly suggests early chemical pneumonitis evolving into aspiration pneumonia, exacerbated by a history of dysphagia and poor dentition. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have permitted unimpeded bacterial proliferation in the dependent lobe, leading to severe sepsis and respiratory failure. An urgent after-hours assessment was strictly required to initiate empirical antibiotics, optimize patient positioning, and prevent further immediate aspiration events.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced oral amoxicillin/clavulanate. Administered supplemental oxygen at 2L/min to maintain saturations >94%. Ordered strict nil by mouth (NBM) until formal speech pathology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of the bed to 45 degrees at all times; maintaining this upright posture utilizes gravity to prevent gastric contents from traveling up the esophagus, directly reducing the frequency of micro-aspiration events.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to Speech Pathology for swallow assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Suction machine placed at bedside.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow morning; low threshold for hospital transfer if oxygen requirements increase.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (000) if the patient develops increased work of breathing, cyanosis, altered conscious state, or fails to maintain oxygen saturations above 90% on home oxygen.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical diagnosis of aspiration pneumonia requires confirmation via chest imaging to visualize dependent lobe consolidation.
Clinical Course: The patient is at high risk for clinical deterioration over the next 48 hours; strict adherence to positioning and feeding restrictions is mandatory.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff must call for immediate emergency transport if the patient's breathing rate exceeds 30 breaths per minute or if they become unresponsive.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics target the specific bacteria from the mouth and stomach that have entered the lungs; withholding food temporarily prevents further lung contamination.
Actionable Steps: Perform rigorous oral care using a chlorhexidine swab twice daily; reducing the bacterial load in the mouth ensures that any accidental aspiration involves fewer harmful bacteria, directly lowering pneumonia severity.

